 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to authorize Yeshiva Shareh Torah to file an application for exemption from real property taxes for certain assessment rolls",
    "basePrintNo": "S3223",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3223-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Taxation",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3223\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to authorize Yeshiva Shareh Torah to file an application for\nexemption from real property taxes for certain assessment rolls\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to permit the City of New York to accept\nlate application from Yeshiva Shareh Torah for certain real property\ntax exemptions for the 1992/93 through 2007/08 assessment rolls and,\nif found eligible, to grant exemptions and to cancel all taxes, fines,\npenalties, and interest and make appropriate correction of the subject\nassessment rolls.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 authorizes the NYC Dept of Finance to accept an application\nfor exemption from real property taxes pursuant to Section 420-a of\nthe real property tax law for the 1992-93 through 2007-08 assessment\nrolls from the not-for-profit corporation Yeshiva Shareh Torah. This\nsection further requests that the Department of Finance review the\napplication as if it had been received on or before the taxable status\ndate established for these assessment rolls. If satisfied that the\nYeshiva Shareh Torah would otherwise have been entitled to said\nexemption, the Commissioner of the Department of Finance, upon\napproval by the City Council of New York City, may grant exemption\nfrom all taxation with respect to the subject tax rolls and cancel\ntaxes, fines, penalties and interest remaining unpaid, and make\nappropriate correction of the subject assessment rolls.\n\nSection 2 is the enacting clause.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nYeshiva Shareh Torah, a not-for-profit corporation, acquired title to\na property in the City of New York, borough of Brooklyn in 1992. The\ncongregation, which had a partial exemption, was granted full tax\nexempt status beginning with the 2008/2009 tax year. As there had been\nno meaningful change in the building's use, the congregation should\nhave been eligible for a full exemption since its inception, and all\ntax charges for prior years should be voided. It is intended by this\nbill to undo the hardships that will be suffered if the organization\nis required to pay the property taxes, interest and penalties\ncurrently due.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo be determined\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
